//! Explicit hostname-to-`IpAddress` resolution (D223-A1, NETWORK.md §14).
//!
//! The module exports exactly two members, both installed by the runtime from
//! the source-backed Core definitions: `Resolver`, the authority-free protocol
//! prototype, and `ResolutionError`, the prototype of every resolution
//! failure. Importing this module creates no Resolver capability, starts no
//! name service and grants no `Network` authority.
//!
//! A concrete Resolver capability is granted only explicitly by the host, as
//! the RootActor initial module's bootstrap-local `resolver` slot. It is
//! independent of `network`: neither implies the other. It is live and never
//! transferable to another Actor or into P.
//!
//! `resolver.lookupAddresses(name)` answers `Future<Array<IpAddress>>`. `name`
//! is a String of ASCII NR-LDH labels or strict IDNA2008 A-labels, ASCII
//! case-insensitive, optionally ending with one root dot; one label is
//! permitted; labels have 1..63 octets and the name at most 253. Unicode
//! U-labels, numeric address text and any other name fail with a fresh
//! `InvalidIOArgument` before any name-service work; no search domain is ever
//! appended. A successful lookup is a fresh frozen Array of 1..64 distinct
//! canonical frozen `IpAddress` values in advisory order. Failures are fresh
//! `ResolutionError` occurrences whose own `kind` is "noAddress",
//! "unavailable", "denied" or "limit" (more than 64 distinct addresses is
//! "limit", never truncated). Use after close fails with `IOLifecycleError`.
//! The lookup Future is an ordinary operation Future, not a Task; cancelling
//! it before its result is published suppresses that result.
//!
//! `resolver.close()` answers `Future<Resolver>` under the ordinary idempotent
//! close rules and closes no Network, connection, HTTP or TLS resource.
//!
//! Resolution never connects. `network.connectTcp` takes only an `IpEndpoint`:
//! choose an address, build `IpEndpoint(address, port)` and connect
//! explicitly. Keep the original hostname as the TLS reference identity and
//! the HTTP `Host` authority.
